"Add a sign-out button and a short Hungarian explanation to the \"Nincs jogosultság\" (no access) page. Small feature.\n\nContext\n- Project: Waldorf community task manager (Next.js, Supabase, trunk-based with squash merges to main, Hungarian UI).\n- Since PR #2 (commit 1070fd6), a signed-in person who has a role but no linked record (for example a parent account with no linked parent record) is sent straight to the \"Nincs jogosultság\" page after sign-in. That page has no sign-out button, so the person is stuck.\n- Seeded test accounts are not affected. Reproduce the case with a test double, or a purpose-made account (for example, remove the parent link of a seeded parent in a throwaway schema).\n- I am an engineering manager with a QA background and no development experience. Explain results in plain language.\n\nWhat to do\n1. Find the page (search app/ and src/ for \"Nincs jogosultság\"). Find how the existing logout button is built on the other pages, and reuse the same server action and component. Do not write a new one.\n2. Minimum good design: a sign-out button, plus a short Hungarian explanation of what to do (contact a group admin). Record your assumptions in the AI-DLC record.\n3. Tests in the same change, including a regression test that fails before the change and passes after it. Keep the 80% coverage floor.\n4. Prettier, ESLint and the strict typecheck must pass. Identifiers in English, user-facing text in Hungarian. No serious or critical accessibility violations on the screen.\n\nVerification\n- Run the change in a real running app: start your own dev server on a port other than 3000, which I use.\n- The local app needs environment variables exported in the terminal and the local database. See README section \"Run the application locally\": load .env.database.local, set APP_SESSION_SECRET, APP_PUBLIC_URL and SMTP_FROM, and run npm run db:start.\n- Show that the no-access page offers sign-out and that signing out works.\n\nRules\n- Do not commit, push or open a PR without asking me first.\n- Never print seeded passwords.\n- When done, explain the result in plain language: what changed, why it matters, what checks you ran, and any limitations."
